UNIT binding_of
LANG js
PRIMARY-SHA256 a9963d3c62e76df3f93e123b62366f6c8cebcfeffe044b7ab3ee0478499b6d3d
SIG export function bindingOf(langText, primaryText)
ABI text = string (UTF-16 code units; an unpaired surrogate is a legal string member); none = null; the record result is a
  fresh plain object with exactly the own keys status, bound and primarySha (text values are strings, none is null). The
  driver calls the named export bindingOf(langText, primaryText) positionally. The artifact is an ES module; it may import
  only the host built-in node:crypto.
ENVELOPE widens the primary to strings holding unpaired surrogate code units, in either argument: OUT-OF-DOMAIN 1-2 of the
  primary hold here and the js-only oracle verifies them. A langText that is not a string throws in the package and stays OUT.
MECHANISM HASH (R4, R5, R7): createHash('sha256').update(s).digest('hex') from node:crypto on the JS string itself — the
  string overload encodes UTF-8 and writes each unpaired surrogate as EF BF BD, which is exactly OUT-OF-DOMAIN 1's legacy
  rule; never pre-encode another way. The OUT-OF-DOMAIN 1 refusal is String.prototype.isWellFormed() false (or an explicit
  scan of code units for unpaired surrogates) checked BEFORE canonical text is built; primarySha is then null and the
  status rows still run.
MECHANISM R1 (the bound) is a line walk over SPLIT-LINES, never a regex search of the whole text; there
  the only separator and trim characters are space and tab (\s, trim() and \S are WRONG for R1: they take U+00A0, U+3000
  and U+2028). The LEGACY parse (R6-R11) is the opposite: SPACE is exactly the set JS regex \s and String.prototype.trim
  use (no u flag), and LINE-BREAK is exactly what an unflagged regex . refuses, so the legacy pins below realize R8, R9 and
  R10 literally, leftmost-match semantics and backtracking included. Legacy pieces (R6) are text.split('\n'), each with one
  trailing \r removed (/\r$/ unflagged); R1 and R3 lines are SPLIT-LINES. Attribute maps keep keyword insertion order (a
  plain object or Map; R11 overwrite is Object.assign or set). Write U+2028 and U+2029 as \u escapes in any literal,
  never raw.
DEPENDS-ON none
PINNED regex ARGUMENT = /^[0-9a-f]{64}$/ CITED R1 (a) — tested on the stripped ARGUMENT, never searched for
PINNED regex NAME-END = /[ \t]/ CITED TABLE PARSE-LINE — the first match ends NAME; no other whitespace separates
PINNED regex SPLIT-LINES = /\r\n|\r|\n/ CITED R1 R3 — CRLF is tried first; R1 and R3 share it
PINNED regex TRAILING-COMMENT = /^(.*?)(?:^|\s)#(?=\s|$)(.*)$/ CITED R8 — CODE is capture 1 with /\s+$/ removed
PINNED regex HEAD = /^(\S+)(?:\s+(.*))?$/ CITED R9 — NAME capture 1, REST capture 2 (absent means empty)
PINNED regex NAME-COLON = /^[A-Z][A-Z0-9-]*:$/ CITED R9 — the colon is dropped
PINNED regex DIRECTIVE = /^(?:[A-Z][A-Z0-9-]*|R\d+)$/ CITED R9 DIRECTIVE-NAME
PINNED regex ATTR = /\s+(CITED|EARNED|ANCHOR)\s+(.+)$/ CITED R10 — applied repeatedly, each match cutting the piece at its index
PROVENANCE rederive@9e2245be5f42 cli/lsp/analyze.mjs bindingOf; cli/sir/v05.mjs boundSha; cli/sir/sir-parse.mjs; cli/sir/legacy-print.mjs. Cut cold 2026-11-02, revised for 9e2245be5f42.
